## INTRODUCTION
Hydrogenation of unsaturated organic compounds is one of the most widely practiced metal-catalyzed reactions in organic synthesis and the chemical industry, with applications in the production of commodity chemicals, pharmaceuticals, and agrochemicals.1 For decades, hydrogenation reactions have relied on precious metal catalysts supported either on solid surfaces2 or by strong field ligands3 to enable two-electron redox chemistryoxidative addition and reductive eliminationthat constitute key bond-breaking and bond-forming steps in the catalytic cycle. However, the low abundance, high price, and inherent toxicity of precious metals have led to intense interest in developing earth-abundant metal catalysts.4 Significant progress has been made in recent years on the development of single-site hydrogenation catalysts based on iron, cobalt, nickel, or copper coordinated with sterically encumbered strong field nitrogen- or phosphorus-donor ligands.5 However, each homogeneous base metal catalyst typically only hydrogenates a narrow class of substrates with limited turnover numbers. Furthermore, few examples of earth-abundant metal-catalyzed hydrogenation of imines and heterocycles exist, and all of them require harsh reaction conditions.
Homogeneous base metal catalysts typically rely on coordination of sterically bulky chelating ligands to prevent the formation of catalytically incompetent oligomeric species by shutting down the intermolecular decomposition pathways.
Such steric protection is important for stabilizing weak-field ligand-coordinated metal catalysts, particularly for late first-row transition metals in a very weak field coordination environment consisting of oxygen-donor atoms.7 However, steric protecting groups often weaken metal−ligand binding and impede the catalytic activity by preventing challenging hydrogenation substrates, such as tri- and tetra- substituted olefins, from accessing the catalytic sites.8 Immobilization of catalytic species in structurally regular porous solid supports can provide catalytic site isolation without relying on bulky ligands, thus offering an alternative route to obtaining highly active base metal catalysts. Significant efforts have been devoted to the development of zeolite-, silica-, or graphene-supported iron- and cobalt-based heterogeneous hydrogenation catalysts6a,9 and bare or protected metallic nanoparticles-based catalysts.10 However, the activities and stability of these heterogeneous hydrogenation catalysts are still far from satisfactory.
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),11 constructed from metal cluster secondary building units (SBUs) and organic linkers, have emerged as a tunable class of porous and crystalline supports for discovering reusable single-site solid catalysts for organic transformations.12 By using premetalated organic struts or via postsynthetic metalation of functionalized bridging linkers,
MOFs have provided a highly tunable platform to engineer single-site solid catalysts for many organic transformations that cannot be performed by traditional porous inorganic materials.13 Furthermore, the SBUs of MOFs consisting of inorganic oxide clusters can be used as oxygen-donor ligands,14 thus providing an unprecedented opportunity for developing robust, site-isolated, base-metal catalysts in a weak field coordination environment. The synthetic tunability of MOFs makes it possible to fine-tune electronic and steric properties of catalytic active sites, whereas the structure regularity and site homogeneity of MOFs greatly facilitate mechanistic studies of MOF-catalyzed reactions.
We recently designed a highly active and chemoselective cobalt-hydride catalyst supported on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{4}$ node of UiO-68 MOF for benzylic $\mathrm{C-H}$ functionalization and olefin hydrogenation.15 Although the potential of $\mathrm{Zr}_{3}–\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ as a ligand site was realized, the $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ site is surrounded by three phenyl rings around $105^{\circ}$ apart from each other, which forms a very sterically encumbered local environment, thus making the $\mathrm{Zr}_{3^{-}}{\mu}_{4^{-}}\mathrm{O-CoH}$ catalyst inactive in the hydrogenation of challenging substrates, such as tetrasubstituted olefins and heterocycles. Design and synthesis of analogous $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based clusters with open environments around the SBUs would make the metal node-supported cobalt catalysts more active in the hydrogenation of challenging substrates and thus potentially applicable in industrial hydrogenations. Here we report a new $\operatorname{Zr}_{8}(\mu_{2}{-}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\mu_{2}{-}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ MOF node with sterically open $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{2}\mathbf{\cdot}\mu_{2}$ -OH ligand sites that support Co-based catalysts for the hydrogenation of a broad range of unsaturated compounds such as olefins, imines, carbonyls, and heterocycles.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis of M-MTBC $(M=Z r$ or Hf) MOFs. $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC was synthesized in $54\%$ yield via a solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime}$ -methanetetrayltetrakis( $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]-4-carboxylic acid) ( $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{MTBC)}$ in DEF using benzoic acid as modulator. A single-crystal X-ray diffraction study of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC indicated that $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC crystallizes in the cubic pm3n space group and revealed the presence of two types of SBUs, $\bar{\mathrm{Zr}_{8}}(\bar{\mu_{2}}–\mathrm{O}\bar{)_{8}}$ $\left(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}\right)_{4}$ and the $\mathrm{\bar{Z}r}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O}{\bf H})_{4}^{-}$ in 1:3 ratio (Figure 1a). To our knowledge, the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}(}\mu_{2}\mathrm{-O)}_{8}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH)}_{4}$ SBU has not been synthesized before as either a discrete cluster or as a structural unit in a MOF.16 In the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}(}\mu_{2}\mathrm{-O)}_{8}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH)}_{4}$ SBU, eight ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Zr}}}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ ions occupy the eight corners of the cube, while eight $\mu_{2}$ -oxo and four $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ occupy the 12 edges of the cube. The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O})_{4}$ $\left(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}\right)_{4}$ unit is isostructural to the SBU of UiO-MOF, with six ${\boldsymbol{Z}}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ ions occupying six corners of an octahedron that are held together by four $\mu_{3}–\phantom{x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x}$ and four $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ groups at eight faces of the octahedron. Solid-state infrared spectrum (IR) spectrum showed the presence of both the $\nu_{\mu_{2}\mathrm{O-H}}$ stretching band at $3737\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $\nu_{\mu_{3}\mathrm{O-H}}$ stretching band at $3639\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 1b). The void space was calculated to be $73.53\%$ by PLATON. The MOF possessed two kinds of trigonal-bipyramid cavities of dimensions $24.9\times21.6\times35.9\mathring{\mathbb{\mathrm{~\AA~}}}$ and $20.8\times20.8\times13.1\mathrm{\AA}$ respectively. $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC showed a type I adsorption $(77\mathrm{K},1\$ bar) with Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $3700~\mathrm{{{m}^{2}/g}}$ (Figure 1c). SEM and TEM images showed cubic particles of $1-3\mu\mathrm{m}$ in length (Figure 1d). The Hf-MTBC analog was synthesized similarly and characterized by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure S9, SI).
Metalation of Zr-MTBC with $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ . $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC was treated with 10 equiv of nBuLi to deprotonate both the $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}^{\prime}s$ in $\mathrm{Zr}_{8}–\mathrm{SBU}$ and the $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}'$ s in $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{SBU}$ , then reacted with a $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ solution in THF to afford $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoCl as a deep-blue solid (Figure 2a).
Both the carboxylate groups and the linkers remained intact during lithiation and metalation, as evidenced by a $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum of the digested $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoCl in $\mathrm{D}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}/\mathrm{DMSO}{-}d_{6}$ (Figure S15, SI) and by the retention of strong carboxylate carbonyl stretching in the IR spectrum (Figure 1b). The disappearance of both the $\nu_{\mu_{2}\mathrm{O-H}}$ band $(3737^{-}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and $\nu_{\mu_{3}\mathrm{O-H}}$ band $(3639~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1})}$ in the IR spectrum indicated that the metalation occurred at both SBU sites (Figure S12, SI). Inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis of the digested MOF revealed complete metalation of all $\mathrm{Zr}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters, corresponding to four Co centers per $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ or $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node. Crystallinity of the MOF was maintained after metalation, as indicated by the similarity between the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MTBC and $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC$\mathbf{CoCl}$ (Figure 2b). However, the coordination environments of the Co centers in $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoCl could not be established by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction due to intrinsic disorder of coordinated cobalt atoms (Figure S16, SI), similar to our previously reported examples.15 We instead used X-ray adsorption spectroscopy (XAS) to investigate Co coordination environments. Four out of six faces of the $\mathsf{\bar{Z}r}_{8}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-O}})_{8}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{4}$ cubic node had a $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ group that could be lithiated and used for Co binding (Figure 2a). We envisioned two different Co coordination modes on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{8}$ node: $\mu_{2}$ -oxide $'\mu_{2}$ -oxo chelation and $\mu_{2}$ -oxide/( $\mu$ -carboxylate)2 tridentate binding. We realized that the $\mu_{2}–\mathrm{oxide}/\mu_{2}$ -oxo chelation binding mode would not be ideal because the $\mu_{2}$ -oxide to $\mu_{2}$ -oxo distance was only $2.35\mathrm{\AA},$ too short for chelation to the same Co center. Such a structural model does not fit the extended X-ray adsorption fine structure (EXAFS) data (Figure S19, SI). In contrast, the $\mu_{2}$ -oxide and two $\mu$ -carboxylate groups could coordinate to the same $C_{0}$ center in a stable conformation, with Co to $\mu_{2}$ -oxide distance of $1.88\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $C_{0}$ to $\mu$ -carboxylate distance of $2.00\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ as indicated by the EXAFS fitting result (Figure 2d). Cobalt coordination on $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ node also adopts a $\mu_{2}$ -oxide/( $\mu$ -carboxylate)2 tridentate mode, identical to that observed in the previously studied UiO-68-CoCl system.15
Activation of Zr-MTBC-CoCl with ${\sf N a B E t}_{3}{\sf H}$ for Olefin Hydrogenation. Treating $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoCl with 5 equiv of $\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ in THF generated the cobalt-hydride species $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH as a black solid. We did not observe the formation of hydrogen gas by GC analysis, indicating $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{Cl}$ metathesis during the activation step. The reaction of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH with 10 equiv of formic acid at room temperature or with excess water at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ readily generated an equivalent amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure S21, SI),17 supporting the identity of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH. XANES analysis of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH suggested a $+2$ oxidation state of $C_{0}$ (Figure 2c). EXAFS fitting on $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH indicated that the Co adopts $\mu_{2}$ -oxide/( $\mu$ -carboxylate)2 tridentate binding mode, with Co to $\mu_{2}$ -oxide distance of $1.83\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and Co to $\eta_{2}$ -carboxylate distance of 1.94, similar to the structure proposed for $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoCl (Figure 2e).
$Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH proved to be a highly active catalyst for hydrogenation of a range of alkenes in THF at room temperature. At a $0.05~\mathrm{mol}~\%$ Co loading, terminal alkenes containing oxo-, carboxy-, pyridyl-, or silyl-functionalitiesallyl ether, allyl acetate, dimethyl itaconate, 2-vinylpyridine, and allyltrimethylsilanewere selectively hydrogenated to afford dipropylether, propylacetate, dimethyl 2-methylsuccinate, 2-ethylpyridine, and propyltrimethylsilane, respectively, in quantitative yields (entries 1−6, Table 1). At a $0.05{-}0.1\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%$ Co loading, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH was also very active in hydrogenation of trisubstituted alkenes such as ethyl-3,3- dimethyl acrylate, $\alpha$ -terpinene, trans- $\alpha$ -methylstilbene, and 1-methyl-1-cyclohexene, and corresponding pure hydrogenated products were obtained in excellent yields by simple filtration of reaction mixtures followed by removal of the volatiles (entries 8−12, Table 1). Impressively, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH completely hydrogenated tetrasubstituted alkenes such as 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene at room temperature within $48\mathrm{{h}}$ to afford $^{2,3}$ -dimethylbutane with a $\mathrm{TON}>8000$ (entry 13, Table 1). Hydrogenation of bulkier tetrasubstituted alkenes such as 2,3,4- trimethylpent-2-ene could also be achieved at elevated temperatures, which facilitated their diffusion through MOF channels as well as the binding and activation of the substrate at the cobaltsite (entry 14, Table 1). It has been previously observed that $\mathrm{Zr}_{3}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{Co}$ site in $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}{\mathbf{-SBU}}$ of UiO-68 is inactive in catalyzing the hydrogenation of bulky and rigid trisubstituted alkenes such as 1-methyl-1-cyclohexene and tetrasubstituted alkenes.15 Therefore, we believe that the hydrogenation of these bulky alkenes occurred exclusively at the $\mathrm{Zr}_{2}\bar{(}\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O})\mathrm{Co}$ sites in $\mathrm{Zr}_{8}–\mathrm{SBUs}$ of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH. The hydrogenation of 6-methyl-5-hepten-2- one to 6-methyl-2-heptanone catalyzed by $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ or $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is a key step to synthesizing dimethyloctenol (DMOE), an important fragrance compound.18 At a $0.5\mathrm{\mol\}\%$ Co loading, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBCCoH also selectively hydrogenated 6-methyl-5-hepten-2-one at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford 6-methyl-2-heptanone in quantitative yield (entry 15, Table 1). Interestingly, 6-methyl-2-heptanol was obtained quantitatively upon heating the reaction mixture at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (entry 16, Table 1).
Importantly, at a $0.1\mathrm{mol}\%$ Co-loading, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH could be recovered and reused at least 5 times for the hydrogenation of 1-methylcyclohexene (Figure 3) without loss of MOF crystallinity (Figure 2b). Excellent yields $(92-100\%)$ of methylcyclohexane were obtained consistently in the reuse experiments with no observation of other byproducts (Figure S25, SI). The PXRD patterns of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH recovered from the first and sixth runs remained unchanged from that of pristine $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH (Figure 2b), indicating the stability of the MOF under reaction conditions. The heterogeneity of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH was confirmed by several experiments. ICP-MS analyses showed that the amounts of Co and $\mathrm{Zr}$ that leached into the supernatant after the first run were only $1.6\%$ and $0.02\%$ , respectively. Moreover, the rate of hydrogenation was unchanged in the presence of mercury, and no additional hydrogenation was observed after removal of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH from the reaction mixture, which ruled out the role of the leached Co-nanoparticles or other Co-species in catalyzing hydrogenation reactions (Figure S24, SI).
Mechanistic Investigation of Zr-MTBC-CoH-Catalyzed Hydrogenation of 1-Methylcyclohexene. As discussed above, the treatment of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MTBC-CoCl with $\mathrm{NaEt_{3}B H}$ in THF generated Zr-MTBC-CoH species, which is likely the active catalyst in the hydrogenation reactions. The EXAFS spectrum of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-Co recovered from hydrogenation of 1-methylcyclohexene showed the absence of Co−Co scattering from Co nanoparticles, ruling out the formation of any Co-nanoparticles during the catalysis (Figure S22, SI). To further investigate the mechanism, the rate law was determined by the method of initial rates ( $\angle15\%$ conversion) in THF at room temperature. In order to avoid complications caused by the presence of two kinds of
Co-centers in $\mathbf{Zr-MTBC-CoH}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , the initial rates were measured for hydrogenation of 1-methylcyclohexene catalyzed by only $\mathrm{Zr}_{2}(\dot{\mu_{3}}{-}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{\bar{C}o}$ sites, since $\mathrm{Zr}_{3}(\mu_{4}\mathrm{-O})\mathrm{CoH}$ at the SBUs of UiO-68 was inactive in hydrogenation of 1-methylcyclohexene. The empirical rate law showed that the initial rates had a first-order dependence on the cobalt concentrations and $P_{\mathrm{H_{2}}}$ (Figure 4a) and a zeroth-order dependence on the alkene concentration (Figures $4\mathsf{a}$ and S26, SI). The activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at the electron-deficient ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ -center via oxidative addition is unlikely. Our kinetic and spectroscopic data thus suggest that the insertion of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ bond of the alkene into the $\mathrm{Co-H}$ bond generates a Co-alkyl intermediate, which undergoes $\sigma$ -bond metathesis with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the turnover limiting step to give an alkane product, simultaneously regenerating the cobalt-hydride species (Figure 4b).
Zr-MTBC-CoH-Catalyzed Hydrogenation of Imines and Carbonyls. Prompted by the hydrogenation of the carbonyl group of 6-methyl-5-hepten-2-one at elevated temperatures, we sought to investigate the hydrogenation of imines and carbonyls. $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH also displayed excellent activity in catalytic hydrogenation of imines (Table 2). Though hydrogenation of imines is an important synthetic route to amines, examples of base metal catalysts for imine hydrogenation are rare.6b
Our imine hydrogenation reactions were performed in toluene at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 40 bar of $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ in the presence of $0.5\mathrm{mol}\%\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBCCoH. N-benzylidenebenzenamine was completely hydrogenated to $N.$ -benzylaniline in $^{\textrm{\scriptsize5h}}$ . The pure product was isolated in $98\%$ yield after simple filtration followed by removal of the volatiles in vacuo (entry 1, Table 1). The $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH recovered after this reaction remained crystalline, as shown by PXRD (Figure 2b), and the leaching of Co and $Z\mathrm{r}$ into the supernatant was $0.23\%$ and $0.08\%$ , respectively. $N\mathrm{.}$ (4-chlorobenzylidene)benzenamine, $N$ -(2-methoxybenzylidene)benzenamine, $N$ -(4-methoxybenzylidene)benzenamine, and $N$ -benzylidenebenzylamine were efficiently reduced within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to afford corresponding $N$ -benzylanilines in excellent yields (entries $2{-}5,$ Table 2). The hydrogenation of trisubstituted imines, such as $(E)$ -N-(1- phenylethylidene)aniline, however, required longer reaction times (entry 7, Table 2), presumably due to the decreased rates of diffusion of the larger substrate and product through the MOF channels and less facile binding and activation of the substrate.
$Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH is also active in catalyzing hydrogenation of carbonyls to their corresponding alcohols in toluene at $\mathsf{90}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .5b,19 At a $0.5\mathrm{mol}\%$ Co loading, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH afforded 1-phenylethanol, 1-(4-chlorophenyl)ethanol, and cyclohexanol from corresponding ketone substrates in good yields (entries 8−10, Table 1). Benzaldehyde was also efficiently reduced to benzyl alcohol in $90\%$ isolated yield.
Zr-MTBC-CoH-Catalyzed Hydrogenation of Heterocycles. The hydrogenation of heterocycles is challenging due to their resonance stabilization and potential poisoning of catalysts by substrates and their products. Although significant progress has been made in developing precious metal-based molecular and heterogeneous catalysts for selective hydrogenation of $N\mathrm{.}$ -heteoarenes such as indoles and quinolines, the advancement of the analogous earth abundant-metal catalysts has lagged behind.6a,20 Catalytic hydrogenation of O-heteroarenes such as furans and benzofurans is also significantly under-developed. Additionally, the hydrogenation of heteroarenes typically requires harsh reaction conditions, high catalyst loadings, and excess additives.
At a $0.5\mathrm{mol}\%$ Co loading, $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH-catalyzed hydrogenation of indole in toluene at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to affords a mixture of indoline and $^{4,5,6,7}$ -tetrahydroindole. Indoline was obtained in $84\%$ isolated yield after preparative thin-layer chromatography (entry 1, Table 3). Interestingly, hydrogenation of 3-methylindole gave 3-methyl-indoline and 3-methyl-4,5,6,7-tetrahydroindole in 46:54 ratio, which indicates that reduction of the phenyl ring is also possible. Hydrogenation of quinolines in toluene at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ gave a mixture of two products, 1,2,3,4- tetrahydroquinoline and $_{5,6,7,8}$ -tetrahydro-quinoline in a 1:1 ratio. Under identical reaction conditions, the selectivity is highly dependent on the substitution of the phenyl ring. Electrondonating substituents at the 6-position of the quinolines favor the hydrogenation of phenyl ring. For example, the 6-methylquinoline, 6-methoxyquinoline, and 2,6-dimethylquinoline were hydrogenated to give 6-methyl-5,6,7,8-tetrahydro-quinoline, 6-methoxy-5,6,7,8-tetrahydro-quinoline, and 2,6-dimethyl5,6,7,8-tetrahydro-quinoline, respectively, as the major products (entries 5−7, Table 3). In contrast, strong electron-withdrawing substituents disfavor the reduction of the phenyl ring. The hydrogenation of 2-methyl-6-fluoro-quinoline afforded 2-methyl-6-fluoro-1,2,3,4,-tetrahydro-quinoline exclusively in $72\%$ yield (entry 8, Table 3). Interestingly, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MTBC-CoH was also a very active catalyst for hydrogenation of benzofuran. At a $0.2\mathrm{mol}\%$ Co loading, benzofuran was completely hydrogenated to 2,3- dihydrobenzofuran in qualitative yield (entry 9, Table 3).
